  "id": "jamaican_maroon_kromanti",
  "name": "Kromanti play (Jamaican Maroon ceremonial music)",
  "family": "global",
  "lineage": "Ceremonial music of the Jamaican Maroons, descendants of Africans who escaped slavery and formed free mountain communities recognised by peace treaties with the British colonial government in the eighteenth century; it is kept by the Windward Maroons of Moore Town, whose heritage UNESCO proclaimed a Masterpiece of the Oral and Intangible Heritage of Humanity, and by the Leeward Maroons of Accompong. In Kromanti play, songs in Kromanti, a ritual language rich in Akan (Twi) words, are led and answered over drumming to call the ancestral spirits, who possess the dancers; the abeng, a side-blown cow horn, carries signals between communities. A generic membrane-drum card stands in for the Maroon drums.",
  "recipe": "classic modern kromanti play, ritual Devotional, Afro-diasporic branch. membrane drum pulse resonant acoustic membrane drum. field recording. ORTF stereo pair, portable field-recorder pre. 24-bit digital.",
